[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: VLAN 10 bridging on the trunk between access switch1 and core mswitch1. Specifically, the VLAN 10 subinterface eth0.10 on mswitch1 is down, breaking the L2 path to the router gateway.

Mechanism: PC4 and PC5 (VLAN 10) can reach each other locally on switch1 (pc5→pc4 ping OK), but cannot ARP their gateway. On pc5, ARP for 172.16.10.1 is incomplete. On router0, ARP for 172.16.10.4 fails, showing the broadcast domain isn’t reaching the router. Laptop0 on VLAN 20 is unaffected because VLAN 20 on the same trunk is operational.

Localization: 
- switch1: eth0.10 (trunk VLAN 10) is UP and forwarding (ip -d link shows eth0.10 state UP, forwarding).
- mswitch1: eth0.10 is DOWN and disabled in br_vlan10 (ip -br link shows eth0.10@eth0 DOWN; ip -d link shows bridge_slave state disabled). Conversely, eth0.20 is UP and forwarding, explaining VLAN 20 health. Toward the router, mswitch1 eth4.10 is UP and forwarding, and router0 eth0.10 is UP with 172.16.10.1/24.

Root cause: The VLAN 10 subinterface eth0.10 on mswitch1 is administratively down/disabled, blocking VLAN 10 across the mswitch1–switch1 trunk.

Corrective fix: On mswitch1, re-enable the subinterface and ensure it remains in br_vlan10:
- ip link set dev eth0.10 up
- verify with brctl show and ip -d link show eth0.10 (state forwarding)
- make configuration persistent per device startup config
After this, ARP and pings to 172.16.10.1 from PC4/PC5 should succeed. 
=== END ===